(*
# Dump Objects
Produces `map/objects.zip`: one compact JSON array per populated map chunk,
named `<plane>-<regionX>-<regionY>.json`, listing every object placement the
map renderer would draw there.

The Java this replaces is `SimbaObjectInfoDumper`, which writes the zip itself
rather than leaving a directory for the CI to compress.

## This artifact deliberately does NOT match the committed zip
Every one of the 48857 entries in `wasp-assets/map/objects.zip` carries the same
`size[2]` (523) and the same `colors`. That is a defect in the Java, not a
property of the data: `SimbaObjectInfoDumper.java:331,350` memoises the computed
geometry under `object.getObjectID()`, and `ObjectDefinition.objectID`
(`ObjectDefinition.java:66`) is declared and **never assigned anywhere in the
repository**. Every lookup and every store therefore uses key 0, so the first
object processed stamps its height and colour list onto all 48857 entries.
`getId()` is the field that actually holds the id; `getObjectID()` is a
different, dead one that Lombok gave a getter to. Full analysis in
`docs/phase2-objects-degeneracy.md`.

This unit emits the correct per-object values, so:

| field | against the committed zip |
|---|---|
| `id`, `name`, `type`, `category`, `actions`, `coordinates`, `size[0]`, `size[1]`, `rotations` | **identical** |
| entry names, entry order, JSON framing | **identical** |
| `size[2]`, `colors` | **different, on purpose** |

`hashes.json` for `objects.zip` will therefore never match the Java pipeline's,
and that is the intended outcome. `dev/tests/413_objects.simba` asserts the
first group exactly and asserts of the second only that it is *not* degenerate -
more than one distinct height, more than one distinct colour set - so a
regression back to a single constant fails loudly instead of quietly restoring
"parity".

## What decides the entry order
Two nested rules, and both have to be computed rather than assumed.

1. **Plane-major.** `for (int i = 0; i < Region.Z; ++i) dumper.mapRegions(i, zip)`
   (`SimbaObjectInfoDumper.java:146`), so the zip is four blocks: every plane-0
   chunk, then every plane-1 chunk, and so on. Measured on the committed file:
   1606 / 650 / 388 / 141.
2. **Java `HashMap` bucket order over region ids**, because the inner loop is
   `for (Region region : regionLoader.getRegions())` and `getRegions()` returns
   `HashMap<Integer, Region>.values()` (`RegionLoader.java:52`) keyed by
   `regionId = (x shl 8) or y`, inserted ascending. See
   {ref}`ObjectDumpTableSize` and {ref}`ObjectDumpBucketOrder`.

   The table size on cache 2625 works out to 4096, which is why the research
   could describe the order as `sort by (regionId mod 4096, regionId)` - but
   4096 only bounds the region count to 1537..3072. **Nothing here hardcodes
   it.** Cross 3072 regions in a future revision and the table becomes 8192 and
   every entry in the file moves.

## Three data-dependent values this unit computes
Hardcoding any of them turns a revision change into a silent whole-file diff.

- **`highestY.baseY`** - `drawBaseY = regionLoader.getHighestY().getBaseY() -
  region.getBaseY()` (`:426`), the max `baseY` over the regions actually loaded.
  It is 12544 on cache 2625, and it is a term in **every Y coordinate in the
  file**. {ref}`TObjectDump.Create` derives it from the idx5 reference table.

  Java loads a region for every idx5 archive whose id is below `MAX_REGION`
  (`RegionLoader.java:70-80`); `loadMapDef` can only return null when the
  archive is absent from the index, and our id list comes *from* the index, so
  the two sets are the same by construction. {ref}`TObjectDump.LoadRegions`
  raises rather than skipping if one of them turns out to have no terrain,
  because that would move `highestY` without anything saying so.

  **No test can tell a hardcoded 12544 from this computation on the caches we
  have.** Both OpenRS2 2625 and 2639 declare the same 2933 regions and the same
  `highestY.baseY` of 12544, so substituting the constant produces byte-identical
  output and `dev/tests/413_objects.simba` passes. That is precisely what makes
  the trap silent: the day a revision adds a region south of row 196, a hardcode
  shifts every Y coordinate in the file and nothing says why. The only defence is
  that the value is derived here rather than written down.
- **The `HashMap` table size**, above.
- **The set of regions**, which is read from idx5 and not assumed to be dense.

## Order-dependent shared state, reproduced rather than avoided
`resolveMorphedObject` (`:243-263`) does more than pick a definition: when it
falls through to a morph target whose category is 0, it writes the *base*
object's category into that target and the write **persists for the rest of the
run** (`:255`). It is a mutation of `ObjectManager`'s shared definition, so a
morphed object's category is decided by whichever base resolved to it first, and
chunks dumped before that point ship a different value from chunks dumped after.
33 object ids in the committed zip appear with two different categories because
of it.

That is why {ref}`TObjectDump.Run` walks planes and regions in exactly the Java's
order instead of the cheaper region-major order: the *selection* a morph makes is
order-independent (`isValidObject` never reads a category), but the value it
emits is not.

## Memoisation: per model, never per entity
The Java bug lives in an entity-level memo, so this unit does not have one.
{ref}`TObjectDump.ModelStat` caches {ref}`TModelStats.OfModel` by **model id**,
which is provably transparent - a model's height and colours are a pure function
of its own bytes, and 15882 objects share far fewer models between them. The
entity-level loop that combines them is re-run per emitted entry, exactly as
`SimbaNPCDumper` does it and exactly as {ref}`TModelStats.OfModels` spells it
out: height from the first model only, colours concatenated across every model
with no cross-model dedup, and no recolour or retexture applied.

## Layout notes
- Entry name is `z + "-" + region.getRegionX() + "-" + region.getRegionY() +
  ".json"` (`:435`), and an empty chunk is **skipped** - `exportEmptyJSONs` is
  `false` (`:70`), so the committed file has 2785 entries for 2933 x 4 = 11732
  possible ones.
- The JSON is `JsonArray.toString()`: compact, no trailing newline, and **not**
  HTML-escaped, because `JsonElement.toString()` builds a bare `JsonWriter`
  whose `htmlSafe` default is false. `<col=00ffff>` and apostrophes go out raw -
  use {ref}`JSONEscape`, not {ref}`JSONEscapeHTML`. Key order is the literal
  insertion order of `:373-411`: `id, name, type, category, actions,
  coordinates, size, rotations, colors`.
- Every number is an integer. Nothing here may ever emit `1.0`.
*)
{$DEFINE WL_CACHE_DUMP_OBJECTS_INCLUDED}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DUMP_COMMON_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE dumpcommon.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DUMP_ZIPWRITER_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE zipwriter.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DUMP_MODELSTATS_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE modelstats.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_TYPES_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../cachetypes.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_STORE_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../store.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_REGION_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../region.simba}{$ENDIF}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DEF_OBJECT_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE ../defs/object.simba}{$ENDIF}

const
  // SimbaObjectInfoDumper.MAP_SCALE - pixels per map square along one axis.
  OBJDUMP_MAP_SCALE: Integer = 4;

  // Tiles per region, and the number of (tile, plane) slots a location can
  // occupy: 64 x 64 x 4 = 16384.
  OBJDUMP_TILES: Integer = 4096;
  OBJDUMP_SLOTS: Integer = 16384;

(*
## ObjectDumpSpread
```pascal
function ObjectDumpSpread(key: Integer): Integer;
```
`java.util.HashMap.hash` for a boxed `Integer` key: `h xor (h >>> 16)`, where an
`Integer`'s own `hashCode` is its value.

Java's `>>>` is the **logical** shift and Lape's `shr` is logical too, so a plain
`shr` is right here - unlike `>>`, which would need an arithmetic helper. Region
ids are non-negative and below 65536 anyway, so every one of them spreads to
itself; the mix only starts to matter above that, and it is written out in full
so a larger id space does not silently break the order.
*)
function ObjectDumpSpread(key: Integer): Integer;
begin
  Result := key xor (key shr 16);
end;

(*
## ObjectDumpTableSize
```pascal
function ObjectDumpTableSize(count: Integer): Integer;
```
The table capacity a default-constructed `java.util.HashMap` ends up with after
`count` insertions: start at 16 with a threshold of `0.75 * capacity`, and double
both the moment the size passes the threshold.

Capacity is what `bucket = spread(key) and (capacity - 1)` is taken modulo, so it
decides the entire entry order of `map/objects.zip`. On cache 2625 the answer is
4096 for 2933 regions - but 4096 only tells you the count was between 1537 and
3072, which is exactly why this is computed. A revision with 3073 regions gets
8192 and a completely different file.

```pascal
WriteLn ObjectDumpTableSize(2933);  // 4096
WriteLn ObjectDumpTableSize(3073);  // 8192
```
*)
function ObjectDumpTableSize(count: Integer): Integer;
var
  size, threshold: Integer;
begin
  Result := 16;
  threshold := 12;   // 0.75 * 16, the DEFAULT_LOAD_FACTOR
  for size := 1 to count do
    if size > threshold then
    begin
      Result *= 2;
      threshold := (Result * 3) div 4;
    end;
end;

(*
## ObjectDumpBucketOrder
```pascal
function ObjectDumpBucketOrder(keys: TIntegerArray): TIntegerArray;
```
Positions into `keys` in the order a `java.util.HashMap` iterates them: buckets
`0 .. capacity-1` ascending and, within a bucket, insertion order - which is the
order `keys` is given in.

Java 8 preserves a bin's relative order across a resize, so the chain never gets
reshuffled and one pass over the final capacity reproduces the whole sequence.
The one thing that would break that is treeification, which reorders a bin into
red-black order once it holds 8 entries at a capacity of 64 or more. It cannot
happen for this key set: with 2933 region ids the fullest bin ever reaches is 6,
and the final table has at most 3 per bin.

Implemented as a stable counting sort over the buckets, so it is one pass over
`keys` plus one over the table rather than a comparison sort.

```pascal
WriteLn ObjectDumpBucketOrder([5, 21, 3, 37, 1]);  // [4, 2, 0, 1, 3]
```
*)
function ObjectDumpBucketOrder(keys: TIntegerArray): TIntegerArray;
var
  start: TIntegerArray;
  capacity, mask, i, b: Integer;
begin
  Result := [];
  if Length(keys) = 0 then Exit;

  capacity := ObjectDumpTableSize(Length(keys));
  mask := capacity - 1;

  // start[b + 1] counts bucket b, then a prefix sum turns it into that
  // bucket's first output slot. SetLength keeps the overlapping prefix of
  // whatever the slot held, hence the `:= []` first.
  start := [];
  SetLength(start, capacity + 1);
  for i := 0 to High(keys) do
  begin
    b := ObjectDumpSpread(keys[i]) and mask;
    Inc(start[b + 1]);
  end;
  for b := 1 to capacity do
    start[b] += start[b - 1];

  SetLength(Result, Length(keys));
  for i := 0 to High(keys) do
  begin
    b := ObjectDumpSpread(keys[i]) and mask;
    Result[start[b]] := i;
    Inc(start[b]);
  end;
end;

// `[a,b,c]`, always integers - never a float, and never an empty pair of
// braces where the Java writes `[]`.
function _ObjDumpInts(values: TIntegerArray): String;
var
  i: Integer;
begin
  Result := '[';
  for i := 0 to High(values) do
  begin
    if i > 0 then Result += ',';
    Result += ToStr(values[i]);
  end;
  Result += ']';
end;

// The `actions` array: every op slot the cache set, in slot order, with the
// holes dropped - `SimbaObjectInfoDumper.java:381-385` skips a null op rather
// than writing one, so the slot indices are lost. Phase 2 measured this against
// all 15882 committed object ids.
function _ObjDumpActions(ops: TEntityOps): String;
var
  i: Integer;
begin
  Result := '[';
  for i := 0 to High(ops.Ops) do
    if ops.Ops[i] <> '' then
    begin
      if Result <> '[' then Result += ',';
      Result += '"' + JSONEscape(ops.Ops[i]) + '"';
    end;
  Result += ']';
end;

// A String as its raw bytes, for TZipWriter.
function _ObjDumpBytes(s: String): TByteArray;
begin
  Result := [];
  if s = '' then Exit;
  SetLength(Result, Length(s));
  Move(s[1], Result[0], Length(s));
end;

type
(*
## TObjectDumpDef
```pascal
TObjectDumpDef = record
  Id: Integer;
  NameJSON, ActionsJSON: String;
  SizeX, SizeY, InteractType, WallOrDoor, Category: Integer;
  Models, ConfigChangeDest: TIntegerArray;
  IsNullName: Boolean;
end;
```
The slice of a {ref}`TObjectDefinition` this dumper reads, with the two string
fields already rendered as JSON so they are escaped once rather than once per
placement.

Holding the full definitions instead would mean 62194 live `TEntityOps` records,
each of which eagerly allocates a 5 x 20 string grid - about six million string
slots for four fields that are actually used.

**`Category` is mutable and shared**, which is the whole point: it stands in for
the `ObjectDefinition` instance `ObjectManager` hands out, and
{ref}`TObjectDump.ResolveMorph` writes to it mid-dump.
*)
  TObjectDumpDef = record
    Id: Integer;
    NameJSON: String;
    ActionsJSON: String;
    SizeX, SizeY: Integer;
    InteractType, WallOrDoor: Integer;
    Category: Integer;
    Models: TIntegerArray;
    ConfigChangeDest: TIntegerArray;
    IsNullName: Boolean;
  end;

(*
## TObjectDumpRegion
```pascal
TObjectDumpRegion = record
  Id, RegionX, RegionY, BaseX, BaseY: Integer;
  Loaded: Boolean;
  Settings: TByteArray;
  Locs: array of TLocation;
  TileXY, PlaneStart: TIntegerArray;
end;
```
One region, kept in the shape the tile loop wants rather than the shape
{ref}`TRegion` decodes into.

The dump visits every region four times, once per plane, so the alternative to
holding this is decoding all 2933 regions four times over - 48 s a pass on this
machine. Only the settings byte and the locations are kept, not the 16384 full
tiles.

`Locs` is sorted by `(tile, plane)`, stably, so a tile's locations for one plane
are a contiguous run. `TileXY` lists the `x * 64 + y` of every tile that has at
least one location, ascending - which is exactly the `localX` outer / `localY`
inner order the Java walks - and `PlaneStart` holds five offsets per such tile:
the start of planes 0..3 and the end of plane 3. A tile with no locations on any
plane can never emit anything, so skipping it is not a shortcut with a behaviour
attached; it turns a 16384-iteration sweep per region-plane into one over the
handful of tiles that can produce output.
*)
  TObjectDumpRegion = record
    Id, RegionX, RegionY, BaseX, BaseY: Integer;
    Loaded: Boolean;
    Settings: TByteArray;
    Locs: array of TLocation;
    TileXY: TIntegerArray;
    PlaneStart: TIntegerArray;
  end;

(*
## TObjectDump
```pascal
TObjectDump = record
  Defs: array of TObjectDumpDef;
  Regions: array of TObjectDumpRegion;
  Order: TIntegerArray;
  TableSize, HighestBaseY: Integer;
  Names, Chunks: TStringArray;
  MorphResolved, CategoryMutations: Integer;
  MutatedIds: TIntegerArray;
end;
```
The whole dump: build it with {ref}`TObjectDump.Create`, run it with
{ref}`TObjectDump.Run`, then read `Names` and `Chunks` - two parallel arrays in
zip-entry order.

`MorphResolved`, `CategoryMutations` and `MutatedIds` are not diagnostics for
their own sake: they are the measurement of how much of the output depends on
`resolveMorphedObject`'s shared-state write, which is the one thing here that is
sensitive to the order regions are walked in.
*)
  TObjectDump = record
    // OBJECT archive, addressed by position; PosOf maps an id to one.
    Defs: array of TObjectDumpDef;
    PosOf: TIntegerArray;
    MaxObjectId: Integer;

    // Model geometry, memoised per model id. See the unit docstring.
    Textures: array of TTextureDefinition;
    ModelHeight: TIntegerArray;
    ModelColors: array of TIntegerArray;
    ModelKnown: TBooleanArray;
    // Read-only profiling of model-cache misses during one dump.
    ProfileModelDecodeMS: Double;
    ProfileModelDecodeCount: Integer;
    ProfileLoadRegionsMS: Double;
    ProfileBuildChunksMS: Double;

    Regions: array of TObjectDumpRegion;
    Order: TIntegerArray;
    TableSize: Integer;
    HighestBaseY: Integer;

    Names: TStringArray;
    Chunks: TStringArray;

    MorphResolved: Integer;
    CategoryMutations: Integer;
    MutatedIds: TIntegerArray;

    // cleanJSON's "have I already emitted this id in this chunk?" lookup.
    // A generation stamp instead of clearing 62201 slots per chunk.
    Stamp: TIntegerArray;
    StampSlot: TIntegerArray;
    Gen: Integer;
  end;

(*
## TObjectDump.DefPos
```pascal
function TObjectDump.DefPos(id: Integer): Integer;
```
Position of object `id` in {ref}`TObjectDump.Defs`.

Raises when the OBJECT archive has no such id. That is not defensive - it is
what the Java does, loudly instead of quietly: `findObject` returns `null` from
a `HashMap` and the very next line dereferences it, so a location naming an
absent object crashes `SimbaObjectInfoDumper` with a `NullPointerException`.
Measured on cache 2625: it never happens, for a location id or for a
`configChangeDest` entry.
*)
function TObjectDump.DefPos(id: Integer): Integer;
begin
  if (id < 0) or (id > Self.MaxObjectId) then
    raise 'DumpObjects: object ' + ToStr(id) + ' is outside the OBJECT archive';
  Result := Self.PosOf[id];
  if Result = -1 then
    raise 'DumpObjects: the OBJECT archive has no object ' + ToStr(id);
end;

(*
## TObjectDump.IsValidObject
```pascal
function TObjectDump.IsValidObject(locType, pos: Integer): Boolean;
```
`SimbaObjectInfoDumper.isValidObject` (`:225-238`), which decides whether a
placement is drawn at all.

Four gates, in order: a wall/door loc type (0..3, or 9) needs
`WallOrDoor <> 0`; `InteractType` must not be 0; the object must have models;
and its name must not be `null` case-insensitively. `TObjectDefinition` stores
an absent model list as an **empty** array - the loader only ever allocates one
from a non-zero length - so `Length(Models) = 0` is Java's
`getObjectModels() == null`.

Reads no category, which is what makes the morph *selection* independent of the
mutation {ref}`TObjectDump.ResolveMorph` performs.
*)
function TObjectDump.IsValidObject(locType, pos: Integer): Boolean;
begin
  if ((locType >= 0) and (locType <= 3)) or (locType = 9) then
    if Self.Defs[pos].WallOrDoor = 0 then Exit(False);

  if Self.Defs[pos].InteractType = 0 then Exit(False);
  if Length(Self.Defs[pos].Models) = 0 then Exit(False);

  Result := not Self.Defs[pos].IsNullName;
end;

(*
## TObjectDump.ResolveMorph
```pascal
function TObjectDump.ResolveMorph(locType, basePos: Integer): Integer;
```
`SimbaObjectInfoDumper.resolveMorphedObject` (`:243-263`): the position of the
definition this placement should be drawn as, or **-1** for "skip this location
entirely".

A valid base object is returned as-is. Otherwise the base's
`configChangeDest` list is walked in order and the first valid morph wins.

**The side effect is contract, not incidental.** When the chosen morph has
category 0 and the base does not, the base's category is written into the
morph's shared definition and stays there for the rest of the run (`:255`). A
morphed object's category is therefore decided by whichever base reached it
first, and chunks dumped earlier ship a different value from chunks dumped
later - 33 ids in the committed zip appear with two categories because of it.
Reproducing it is why {ref}`TObjectDump.Run` keeps the Java's plane-major walk.

Each id can be mutated at most once: the guard only fires while the target's
category is still 0, and the write always makes it non-zero.
*)
function TObjectDump.ResolveMorph(locType, basePos: Integer): Integer;
var
  i, id, pos: Integer;
begin
  if Self.IsValidObject(locType, basePos) then Exit(basePos);

  // A null configChangeDest is an empty array here; the loader only allocates
  // it from opcode 77 or 92, both of which give it at least two entries.
  if Length(Self.Defs[basePos].ConfigChangeDest) = 0 then Exit(-1);

  for i := 0 to High(Self.Defs[basePos].ConfigChangeDest) do
  begin
    id := Self.Defs[basePos].ConfigChangeDest[i];
    if id = -1 then Continue;

    pos := Self.DefPos(id);
    if not Self.IsValidObject(locType, pos) then Continue;

    Inc(Self.MorphResolved);
    if (Self.Defs[pos].Category = 0) and (Self.Defs[basePos].Category <> 0) then
    begin
      Self.Defs[pos].Category := Self.Defs[basePos].Category;
      Inc(Self.CategoryMutations);
      Self.MutatedIds += id;
    end;
    Exit(pos);
  end;

  Result := -1;
end;

(*
## TObjectDump.ModelStat
```pascal
function TObjectDump.ModelStat(var store: TCacheStore; model: Integer): TModelStats;
```
{ref}`TModelStats.OfModel` for `model`, memoised by model id.

This is the only memo in the unit, and it is a different thing from the one that
broke the Java: a model's height and colour list are a pure function of that
model's own bytes, so caching them by id cannot change any result. The Java's
cache was keyed by an *entity* field that was never assigned, which is a
correctness bug wearing a performance bug's clothes. The entity-level loop above
it is deliberately re-run every time.

Without this the 15882 objects the dump emits would decode the same shared
models tens of thousands of times over.
*)
function TObjectDump.ModelStat(var store: TCacheStore; model: Integer): TModelStats;
var
  t: Double;
begin
  if (model >= 0) and (model <= High(Self.ModelKnown)) and Self.ModelKnown[model] then
  begin
    Result.Height := Self.ModelHeight[model];
    Result.Colors := Self.ModelColors[model];
    Exit;
  end;

  t := PerformanceTime();
  Result := TModelStats.OfModel(store, Self.Textures, model);
  Self.ProfileModelDecodeMS += PerformanceTime() - t;
  Inc(Self.ProfileModelDecodeCount);

  if (model >= 0) and (model <= High(Self.ModelKnown)) then
  begin
    Self.ModelKnown[model] := True;
    Self.ModelHeight[model] := Result.Height;
    Self.ModelColors[model] := Result.Colors;
  end;
end;

(*
## TObjectDump.EntityStats
```pascal
function TObjectDump.EntityStats(var store: TCacheStore; models: TIntegerArray): TModelStats;
```
The `height` and `colors` one object contributes, from its `ObjectModels` list.

Byte for byte the loop {ref}`TModelStats.OfModels` spells out - height from the
first model and only the first, colours concatenated in array order with the
per-model dedup already applied and no cross-model dedup - just routed through
{ref}`TObjectDump.ModelStat` so a shared model is decoded once. Kept here rather
than calling `OfModels` directly for that one reason; the arithmetic is
identical.

`Result` is a record and is zero-filled per call, so `Height` already starts at
0 and `Colors` already starts empty - the two seeds the Java writes by hand.
Appending to `Result.Colors` reallocates, so the memo's array is never mutated
through the reference this hands out.
*)
function TObjectDump.EntityStats(var store: TCacheStore; models: TIntegerArray): TModelStats;
var
  i: Integer;
  one: TModelStats;
begin
  for i := 0 to High(models) do
  begin
    one := Self.ModelStat(store, models[i]);
    if Result.Height = 0 then Result.Height := one.Height;
    Result.Colors := Result.Colors + one.Colors;
  end;
end;

(*
## TObjectDump.Create
```pascal
function TObjectDump.Create(var store: TCacheStore): TObjectDump; static;
```
Reads everything that does not depend on which regions are dumped: the whole
OBJECT archive, the texture table, the region id set, and from that set the
`HashMap` table size, the entry order and `highestY.baseY`.

Cheap enough to do unconditionally - the 62194 object definitions decode in
under a second on cache 2625 - and it deliberately does **not** load any region.
{ref}`TObjectDump.LoadRegions` does that, so a caller can dump a subset without
changing a single coordinate: everything the coordinate maths needs comes from
the id set, not from the regions that happen to be loaded.

```pascal
store := TCacheStore.Create(cacheDir);
dump := TObjectDump.Create(store);
dump.Run(store, []);
```
*)
function TObjectDump.Create(var store: TCacheStore): TObjectDump; static;
var
  table: TCacheRefTable;
  files: array of TByteArray;
  ids, regionIds: TIntegerArray;
  obj: TObjectDefinition;
  a, i, n: Integer;
begin
  // ---- OBJECT archive ------------------------------------------------------
  table := store.GetRefTable(Ord(EIndexType.CONFIGS));
  a := table.IndexOfArchive(Ord(EConfigType.OBJECTS));
  if a = -1 then
    raise 'DumpObjects: this cache has no CONFIGS/OBJECT archive';

  ids := table.Archives[a].FileIds;
  files := store.ReadFiles(Ord(EIndexType.CONFIGS), Ord(EConfigType.OBJECTS), []);
  if Length(files) <> Length(ids) then
    raise 'DumpObjects: the OBJECT archive holds ' + ToStr(Length(files)) +
          ' files but its reference table names ' + ToStr(Length(ids));

  for i := 0 to High(ids) do
    if ids[i] > Result.MaxObjectId then Result.MaxObjectId := ids[i];

  Result.PosOf := [];
  SetLength(Result.PosOf, Result.MaxObjectId + 1);
  for i := 0 to High(Result.PosOf) do Result.PosOf[i] := -1;

  SetLength(Result.Defs, Length(ids));
  for i := 0 to High(ids) do
  begin
    obj := TObjectDefinition.Load(ids[i], files[i]);

    Result.Defs[i].Id := obj.Id;
    // JsonElement.toString(), so no HTML-safe escaping: 251 raw '<', 431 raw
    // apostrophes in the committed zip and not one \uXXXX anywhere.
    Result.Defs[i].NameJSON := '"' + JSONEscape(obj.Name) + '"';
    Result.Defs[i].IsNullName := SameText(obj.Name, 'null');
    Result.Defs[i].ActionsJSON := _ObjDumpActions(obj.Ops);
    Result.Defs[i].SizeX := obj.SizeX;
    Result.Defs[i].SizeY := obj.SizeY;
    Result.Defs[i].InteractType := obj.InteractType;
    Result.Defs[i].WallOrDoor := obj.WallOrDoor;
    Result.Defs[i].Category := obj.Category;
    Result.Defs[i].Models := obj.ObjectModels;
    Result.Defs[i].ConfigChangeDest := obj.ConfigChangeDest;

    Result.PosOf[ids[i]] := i;
  end;

  Result.Stamp := [];
  Result.StampSlot := [];
  SetLength(Result.Stamp, Result.MaxObjectId + 1);
  SetLength(Result.StampSlot, Result.MaxObjectId + 1);

  // ---- textures and the per-model memo -------------------------------------
  Result.Textures := ModelStatsTextures(store);

  n := store.ArchiveCount(Ord(EIndexType.MODELS));
  Result.ModelHeight := [];
  Result.ModelColors := [];
  Result.ModelKnown := [];
  SetLength(Result.ModelHeight, n);
  SetLength(Result.ModelColors, n);
  SetLength(Result.ModelKnown, n);

  // ---- regions -------------------------------------------------------------
  // RegionLoader walks 0 .. MAX_REGION-1 and keeps every archive it finds, so
  // the loaded set is exactly the idx5 ids below that bound.
  table := store.GetRefTable(Ord(EIndexType.MAPS));
  if table.Named then
    raise 'DumpObjects: index 5 is named (an OpenRS2-style dump); only the ' +
          'unnamed live-cache layout is implemented';

  for i := 0 to High(table.Archives) do
    if (table.Archives[i].Id >= 0) and (table.Archives[i].Id < REGION_MAX_ID) then
      regionIds += table.Archives[i].Id;

  if Length(regionIds) = 0 then
    raise 'DumpObjects: index 5 declares no regions';

  SetLength(Result.Regions, Length(regionIds));
  for i := 0 to High(regionIds) do
  begin
    Result.Regions[i].Id := regionIds[i];
    Result.Regions[i].RegionX := RegionXOf(regionIds[i]);
    Result.Regions[i].RegionY := RegionYOf(regionIds[i]);
    Result.Regions[i].BaseX := Result.Regions[i].RegionX shl 6;
    Result.Regions[i].BaseY := Result.Regions[i].RegionY shl 6;

    // calculateBounds' highestY, and the only one of the four bounds that
    // reaches the output: drawBaseY = highestY.baseY - region.baseY.
    if Result.Regions[i].BaseY > Result.HighestBaseY then
      Result.HighestBaseY := Result.Regions[i].BaseY;
  end;

  Result.TableSize := ObjectDumpTableSize(Length(regionIds));
  Result.Order := ObjectDumpBucketOrder(regionIds);
end;

(*
## TObjectDump.LoadRegions
```pascal
procedure TObjectDump.LoadRegions(var store: TCacheStore; only: TIntegerArray);
```
Decodes the regions the run will walk and indexes each one by tile and plane.

`only` is a list of region ids; **empty means every region**. A subset changes
nothing about coordinates or entry order - both come from the full id set
{ref}`TObjectDump.Create` read - so it is a way to test a few chunks quickly,
not a different dump. The one thing it does change is how much of
`resolveMorphedObject`'s category mutation has happened by the time a given
chunk is written.

A declared region that decodes without terrain **raises**. Java would simply not
have that region in its map, which would move `highestY` and shift every Y
coordinate in the file; that is not a thing to discover from a diff.
*)
procedure TObjectDump.LoadRegions(var store: TCacheStore; only: TIntegerArray);
var
  wanted: TBooleanArray;
  start, fill: TIntegerArray;
  region: TRegion;
  sorted: array of TLocation;
  filtering: Boolean;
  i, j, k, b, t, used: Integer;
begin
  filtering := Length(only) > 0;
  if filtering then
  begin
    wanted := [];
    SetLength(wanted, REGION_MAX_ID);
    for i := 0 to High(only) do
    begin
      if (only[i] < 0) or (only[i] >= REGION_MAX_ID) then
        raise 'DumpObjects: region ' + ToStr(only[i]) + ' is not a region id';
      wanted[only[i]] := True;
    end;
  end;

  for i := 0 to High(Self.Regions) do
  begin
    if filtering and not wanted[Self.Regions[i].Id] then Continue;

    region := TRegion.Load(store, Self.Regions[i].Id, []);
    if not region.HasTerrain then
      raise 'DumpObjects: idx5 declares region ' + ToStr(Self.Regions[i].Id) +
            ' but it decodes with no terrain; that region is in the Java''s ' +
            'bounds calculation and its absence would move every Y coordinate';

    Self.Regions[i].Loaded := True;

    Self.Regions[i].Settings := [];
    SetLength(Self.Regions[i].Settings, REGION_Z * REGION_X * REGION_Y);
    for j := 0 to High(Self.Regions[i].Settings) do
      Self.Regions[i].Settings[j] := region.Tiles[j].Settings;

    // ---- index the locations by (tile, plane) -------------------------------
    // Java rebuilds a HashMap<Long, List<Location>> per region per plane; the
    // key is the absolute position, which is the region-local one plus a
    // constant, so a flat (x * 64 + y) * 4 + plane slot is the same partition.
    // Counting sort, stable, so a slot's locations keep cache order - which is
    // the order LocationsLoader produced them in and the order the Java's
    // ArrayList holds them in.
    start := [];
    SetLength(start, OBJDUMP_SLOTS + 1);
    for j := 0 to High(region.Locations) do
    begin
      b := ((region.Locations[j].X * REGION_Y) + region.Locations[j].Y) * REGION_Z +
           region.Locations[j].Plane;
      Inc(start[b + 1]);
    end;
    for b := 1 to OBJDUMP_SLOTS do
      start[b] += start[b - 1];

    fill := Copy(start);
    sorted := [];
    SetLength(sorted, Length(region.Locations));
    for j := 0 to High(region.Locations) do
    begin
      b := ((region.Locations[j].X * REGION_Y) + region.Locations[j].Y) * REGION_Z +
           region.Locations[j].Plane;
      sorted[fill[b]] := region.Locations[j];
      Inc(fill[b]);
    end;
    Self.Regions[i].Locs := sorted;

    // Tiles that hold at least one location on any plane, ascending in
    // x * 64 + y - which is the localX-outer, localY-inner order the Java walks.
    // First count, then allocate the two compact indices once.  `+=` creates
    // and copies a new dynamic array for every populated tile (and five more
    // times for its plane boundaries); across 2,933 regions that work showed
    // up in the region-projection profile.  The two-pass form keeps exactly
    // the same ascending tile order and boundary values without reallocating.
    used := 0;
    for t := 0 to OBJDUMP_TILES - 1 do
    begin
      k := t * REGION_Z;
      if start[k + REGION_Z] <> start[k] then Inc(used);
    end;
    Self.Regions[i].TileXY := [];
    Self.Regions[i].PlaneStart := [];
    SetLength(Self.Regions[i].TileXY, used);
    SetLength(Self.Regions[i].PlaneStart, used * (REGION_Z + 1));

    used := 0;
    for t := 0 to OBJDUMP_TILES - 1 do
    begin
      k := t * REGION_Z;
      if start[k + REGION_Z] = start[k] then Continue;
      Self.Regions[i].TileXY[used] := t;
      for b := 0 to REGION_Z do
        Self.Regions[i].PlaneStart[used * (REGION_Z + 1) + b] := start[k + b];
      Inc(used);
    end;
  end;
end;

(*
## TObjectDump.BuildChunk
```pascal
function TObjectDump.BuildChunk(var store: TCacheStore; r, z: Integer): String;
```
The JSON array for one chunk - region `r` of {ref}`TObjectDump.Regions`, plane
`z` - or `''` when it holds nothing, which is the case the Java skips instead of
writing `[]` (`exportEmptyJSONs` is false).

Three pieces of `mapObjects` (`:277-417`) are load-bearing:

1. **Which locations a tile contributes.** The bridge bit of plane 1 shifts the
   plane a tile reads from; a `settings and 24` on plane `z` suppresses the
   tile's own locations; and a `settings and 8` on plane `z + 1` pulls that
   plane's locations down onto this one. Plane locations are emitted before
   pushed-down ones (`:320`).
2. **The image-space transform** (`:355-369`), with `drawBaseX` the region's own
   `baseX` and `drawBaseY` the *inverted* `highestY.baseY - baseY`. Y is flipped
   because image space runs top-left and game space bottom-left. The rotated
   branch only applies to non-square objects.
3. **`cleanJSON`** (`:203-223`), merging on `id` **alone**: the first placement
   of an object in a chunk fixes its `name`, `type`, `category`, `size` and
   `colors`, and every later one only appends a coordinate and a rotation. A
   second placement with a different loc type silently loses that type. Entry
   order is order of first occurrence.

Because the merge keeps only the first placement's geometry, the model work runs
once per emitted entry rather than once per location.
*)
function TObjectDump.BuildChunk(var store: TCacheStore; r, z: Integer): String;
var
  settings: TByteArray;
  locs: array of TLocation;
  tileXY, planeStart: TIntegerArray;
  heads, coords, rots, sizes, colours: TStringArray;
  st: TModelStats;
  drawBaseX, drawBaseY, count: Integer;
  i, t, localX, localY, plane, pass, lo, hi, k: Integer;
  tileZ, setZ, locType, basePos, pos, id, slot: Integer;
  sizeX, sizeY, rotation, rotOut, x, y, xs, ys, cx, cy: Integer;
begin
  if not Self.Regions[r].Loaded then Exit('');
  if Length(Self.Regions[r].TileXY) = 0 then Exit('');

  // Hoisted: a dynamic-array assignment shares by reference, and reaching
  // through Self.Regions[r] per tile is the difference between one indexed
  // read and three.
  settings := Self.Regions[r].Settings;
  locs := Self.Regions[r].Locs;
  tileXY := Self.Regions[r].TileXY;
  planeStart := Self.Regions[r].PlaneStart;

  drawBaseX := Self.Regions[r].BaseX;
  drawBaseY := Self.HighestBaseY - Self.Regions[r].BaseY;

  Inc(Self.Gen);
  count := 0;

  for i := 0 to High(tileXY) do
  begin
    t := tileXY[i];
    localX := t div REGION_Y;
    localY := t mod REGION_Y;

    // getTileSetting(1, x, y) - always plane 1, whatever z is.
    tileZ := z;
    if (settings[(REGION_X * REGION_Y) + (localX * REGION_Y) + localY] and 2) <> 0 then
      Inc(tileZ);

    for pass := 0 to 1 do
    begin
      if pass = 0 then
      begin
        setZ := settings[(z * REGION_X * REGION_Y) + (localX * REGION_Y) + localY];
        if (setZ and 24) <> 0 then Continue;
        plane := tileZ;
      end
      else
      begin
        if z >= REGION_Z - 1 then Continue;
        setZ := settings[((z + 1) * REGION_X * REGION_Y) + (localX * REGION_Y) + localY];
        if (setZ and 8) = 0 then Continue;
        plane := tileZ + 1;
      end;

      // tileZ can reach 4 and tileZ + 1 can reach 5; no location decodes above
      // plane 3, so those lookups are empty in the Java too.
      if (plane < 0) or (plane >= REGION_Z) then Continue;

      lo := planeStart[i * (REGION_Z + 1) + plane];
      hi := planeStart[i * (REGION_Z + 1) + plane + 1];

      for k := lo to hi - 1 do
      begin
        locType := locs[k].LocType;
        basePos := Self.DefPos(locs[k].Id);
        pos := Self.ResolveMorph(locType, basePos);
        if pos = -1 then Continue;

        rotation := locs[k].Orientation;
        sizeX := Self.Defs[pos].SizeX;
        sizeY := Self.Defs[pos].SizeY;

        x := (drawBaseX + localX) * OBJDUMP_MAP_SCALE;
        y := (drawBaseY + (REGION_Y - sizeY - localY)) * OBJDUMP_MAP_SCALE;

        xs := sizeX * OBJDUMP_MAP_SCALE;
        ys := sizeY * OBJDUMP_MAP_SCALE;

        cx := x + xs div 2 - 2;
        cy := y + ys div 2;

        if (sizeX <> sizeY) and ((rotation = 1) or (rotation = 3)) then
        begin
          cx := x + ys div 2 - 2;
          cy := y + ys - xs div 2;
        end;

        rotOut := rotation;
        if sizeX = sizeY then rotOut := 0;

        id := Self.Defs[pos].Id;
        if Self.Stamp[id] = Self.Gen then
        begin
          // cleanJSON: same id, so only the coordinate and the rotation land.
          slot := Self.StampSlot[id];
          coords[slot] += ',[' + ToStr(cx) + ',' + ToStr(cy) + ']';
          rots[slot] += ',' + ToStr(rotOut);
          Continue;
        end;

        Self.Stamp[id] := Self.Gen;
        Self.StampSlot[id] := count;

        st := Self.EntityStats(store, Self.Defs[pos].Models);

        heads += '{"id":' + ToStr(id) +
                 ',"name":' + Self.Defs[pos].NameJSON +
                 ',"type":' + ToStr(locType) +
                 ',"category":' + ToStr(Self.Defs[pos].Category) +
                 ',"actions":' + Self.Defs[pos].ActionsJSON;
        coords += '[' + ToStr(cx) + ',' + ToStr(cy) + ']';
        rots += ToStr(rotOut);
        sizes += '[' + ToStr(sizeX) + ',' + ToStr(sizeY) + ',' + ToStr(st.Height) + ']';
        colours += _ObjDumpInts(st.Colors);
        Inc(count);
      end;
    end;
  end;

  if count = 0 then Exit('');

  Result := '[';
  for i := 0 to count - 1 do
  begin
    if i > 0 then Result += ',';
    Result += heads[i] +
              ',"coordinates":[' + coords[i] + ']' +
              ',"size":' + sizes[i] +
              ',"rotations":[' + rots[i] + ']' +
              ',"colors":' + colours[i] + '}';
  end;
  Result += ']';
end;

(*
## TObjectDump.Run
```pascal
procedure TObjectDump.Run(var store: TCacheStore; only: TIntegerArray);
```
Walks every plane and every region and fills `Names` and `Chunks` - two parallel
arrays in zip-entry order.

`only` is passed straight to {ref}`TObjectDump.LoadRegions`; empty means the
whole map.

The walk is **plane-major over regions in `HashMap` bucket order**, which is the
Java's and is not negotiable for two independent reasons: it is the entry order
of the zip, and it is the order `resolveMorphedObject`'s category mutation
happens in. Region-major would be one region decode instead of four and would
still produce the right coordinates - and the wrong categories.

**One run per {ref}`TObjectDump.Create`.** The category mutations
{ref}`TObjectDump.ResolveMorph` performs are not undone, so a second `Run` on
the same record starts from a definition table that is already partly mutated
and produces different categories - the same thing a second dump against one
live `ObjectManager` would do in Java. Build a fresh dump instead.
*)
procedure TObjectDump.Run(var store: TCacheStore; only: TIntegerArray);
var
  t: Double;
  z, i, r: Integer;
  text: String;
begin
  t := PerformanceTime();
  Self.LoadRegions(store, only);
  Self.ProfileLoadRegionsMS += PerformanceTime() - t;

  Self.Names := [];
  Self.Chunks := [];

  // Gen is deliberately NOT reset: `Stamp` still holds the generation numbers
  // the previous run wrote, and restarting the counter would make a stale slot
  // read as current. It only ever counts up.

  t := PerformanceTime();
  for z := 0 to REGION_Z - 1 do
    for i := 0 to High(Self.Order) do
    begin
      r := Self.Order[i];
      if not Self.Regions[r].Loaded then Continue;

      text := Self.BuildChunk(store, r, z);
      if text = '' then Continue;

      Self.Names += ToStr(z) + '-' + ToStr(Self.Regions[r].RegionX) + '-' +
                    ToStr(Self.Regions[r].RegionY) + '.json';
      Self.Chunks += text;
    end;
  Self.ProfileBuildChunksMS += PerformanceTime() - t;
end;

(*
## DumpObjects
```pascal
procedure DumpObjects(var store: TCacheStore; zipPath: String);
```
Writes the whole `map/objects.zip` for `store`: 2785 chunks on cache 2625, in
the Java's entry order, with correct per-object heights and colours.

**The container's bytes will not match the committed zip, and neither will two
of its fields.** `TZipWriter` pins its own timestamps and Simba's deflate
compresses harder than the JDK's, so the archive is never byte-comparable; and
`size[2]` and `colors` are deliberately right here and wrong there. See the unit
docstring. `dev/tests/413_objects.simba` is what checks the rest.

```pascal
store := TCacheStore.Create(cacheDir);
DumpObjects(store, dump.Path('map/objects.zip'));
```
*)
procedure DumpObjects(var store: TCacheStore; zipPath: String);
var
  dump: TObjectDump;
  zip: TZipWriter;
  t, createMS, runMS, writeMS: Double;
  i: Integer;
begin
  t := PerformanceTime();
  dump := TObjectDump.Create(store);
  createMS := PerformanceTime() - t;
  t := PerformanceTime();
  dump.Run(store, []);
  runMS := PerformanceTime() - t;

  t := PerformanceTime();
  zip.Init();
  for i := 0 to High(dump.Names) do
    zip.AddBytes(dump.Names[i], _ObjDumpBytes(dump.Chunks[i]));
  zip.Save(zipPath);
  writeMS := PerformanceTime() - t;
  WriteLn('DumpObjects: create ', Round(createMS), ' ms, regions/json ',
          Round(runMS), ' ms, unique model decodes ', dump.ProfileModelDecodeCount,
          ' in ', Round(dump.ProfileModelDecodeMS), ' ms, zip ', Round(writeMS), ' ms');
  WriteLn('DumpObjects: region projection ', Round(dump.ProfileLoadRegionsMS),
          ' ms, chunk/json loop ', Round(dump.ProfileBuildChunksMS), ' ms');
end;
